## a b s t r a c t
The development of high-performance humidity sensors is of great significance to explore their practical applications in the fields of environment, energy saving and safety monitoring. Herein, a flexible, noncontact and multifunctional humidity sensor based on two-dimensional Co-metal organic frameworks (Co-MOF) nanosheets is proposed, which is fabricated by simple bottom-up synthesis method. Furthermore, environmentally friendly, renewable and abundant biomass phytic acid (PA) is modified on the surface of Co-MOF nanosheets, which releases free protons being capable of etching the framework of MOF to improve the hydrophilicity and conductivity of MOF. Compared with Co-MOF-based sensor, the Co-MOF@PA-based sensor exhibits significantly enhanced sensitivity and broadened response range within $23\mathrm{-}95\%$ relative humidity (RH). The humidity sensor has an excellent humidity sensing response over $2\times10^{3}$ . The Co-MOF@PA-based sensor shows good flexibility and humidity sensing properties, endowing it with multifunctional applications in real-time facial respiration monitoring, skin humidity perception, cosmetic moisturizing evaluation and fruit freshness testing. Four respiration patterns, including slow breath, deep breath, normal breath and fast breath are wirelessly monitored in real time by Co-MOF@PA-based sensor and recorded by mobile phone software. The research work presents potential applications in human–machine interactions (HMI) devices in future. $\circledcirc$ 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
Humidity measurement plays an important role in the fields of environmental monitoring, human physiological activity, precision instrument protection and food security and so on [1–4]. Recently, humidity sensors have aroused a lot of attention because of their applications in breath monitoring, non-contact switch and baby diaper wetting monitoring [5–8]. In comparison with the contact type sensors, such as strain sensors, temperature sensors, pressure sensors and so on, the humidity sensor can sense the humidity change of the surrounding environment by a non-contact way. The non-contact sensing process can avoid the mechanical touch and bacteria transmission, and be adapted to the hazardous environments [9]. Moreover, the humidity sensor can effectively utilize the moisture of human body and food as the signal source to realize wide range and non-contact sense. Therefore, it is necessary to fabricate high performance humidity sensors with excellent flexibility, wide response range and high sensitivity to meet the requirement of above special applications.
Humidity sensors have gained tractions and developed rapidly based on different materials, such as metal oxide [10,11], carbon materials [12,13] and polymers [14,15]. Metal oxide-based humidity sensors demonstrate good chemical stability and thermal stability. However, heating equipment is required during dehydration. In the meantime, polymer-based humidity sensors have excellent humidity sensitive properties, but the poor stability limits their applications. MOF is a kind of organic–inorganic hybrid material, which is formed by connecting metal ions and organic ligands in a network structure. Compared to 3D bulk MOF, 2D MOF nanosheets have superior and tunable properties, such as large specific surface area, adjustable pore size, diverse structures, high thermal and mechanical stability[16], which show great potential in the application of moisture detection [17,18]. Achmann et al. have prepared humidity sensors based on Fe-1,3,5-ben zenetricarboxylate-MOF (Fe-BTC), which exhibited excellent humidity sensing performance at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ [19]. Su et al. explored the influence of alkali salts and precious metals on the humidity sensitivity of MOF films, by adding KOH and Au nanoparticles (NPs). The sensitivity and linearity of the flexible impedance humidity sensor based on the $\mathbf{Mg}$ -MOF film were improved [20]. Rauf et al. deposited MIL-96(Al) MOFs onto interdigitated electrode-based fabrics to prepare a humidity sensor, which performed high selectivity for humidity detection, about 0.6 fF per $\%$ RH with around $0.71\%$ RH limit of detection [21]. Seo et al. prepared humidity sensor by using MIL-100 (Fe) and polyaniline (PA-MIL-100 (Fe)) nanocomposites, which showed high sensitivity and rapid response behavior in a large humidity range [22].
Herein, a multifunctional flexible humidity sensor based on CoMOF is proposed, and then modified by PA to improve the hydrophilicity and conductivity of Co-MOF. The Co-MOF@PAbased sensor exhibits excellent humidity sensing properties, with the response of more than $10^{3}$ and humidity range from 23 to $95\%$ RH. In addition, the flexible Co-MOF@PA-based sensor shows superior repeatability after being bent under $45^{\circ}$ , which exhibits its potential in the field of wearable electronics. Remarkably, the Co-MOF $@$ PA-based sensor with superior sensing performance demonstrates multifunctional applications in the monitoring of breath rate, non-contact subtle humidity sensing on the surface of skin, fruit freshness, and moisturizing effect of cosmetics. This work provides an effective strategy for the design of sensing materials and thus improves the sensing performance of humidity sensor for body and environment monitoring.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of 2D Co-MOF and 2D Co-MOF@PA
(1) Synthesis of 2D Co-MOF: The 2D Co-MOF was synthesized by a facile bottom-up hydrothermal route [23]. $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3}){\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(22~\mathrm{mg})$ , $^{4,\acute{4}}$ -bipyridine $\mathrm{7.8~mg},$ and polyvinylpyrrolidone $(50~\mathrm{mg})$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) and ethanol mixture with volume ratio of 3:1 in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ round-bottom flask. Then, $20\mathrm{\mg\5,10,15,20}.$ -tetra kis(4-carboxyl-phenyl)-porphyrin (TCPP) was dissolved in DMF and ethanol mixture with volume ratio of 3:1. After that, the two parts of the solution were mixed together, following with ultrasonic treatment for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the mixture solution was heated to $80^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in a oil bath pan. Co-MOF were obtained by washing with distilled water and freeze-drying at $-45^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
(2) Synthesis of 2D $C o{-}M O F@P A$ : Co-MOF $\mathrm{20mg},$ and PA $(0.36~\mathrm{mL})$ were dissolved in distilled water $\mathrm{.10~mL}$ in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial, following with ultrasonic treatment for $40~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the mixed solution was transferred into a $30\mathrm{\mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and reacted at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting product was obtained by using the above method. The specific preparation conditions of the two Co-MOF samples are shown in Scheme 1.
## 2.2. Materials characterization
The surface morphologies of the samples were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Talos F200X). The acceleration voltage of FEI Talos F200X is $300~\mathrm{kV}$ Elemental mapping patterns were obtained via energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS), which were connected with TEM (FEI Talos F200X). The elementary compositions of samples were analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ThermoFischer ESCALAB 250Xi). The ultimate energy resolution is $0.43\ \mathrm{\eV}$ and the energy analysis range is 0–5000 eV.
## 2.3. Humidity sensing measurement
The humidity sensors consist of sensing film and substrate. Firstly, the ceramic substrates were washed in alcohol with ultrasonic treatment. The powder of Co-MOF and Co-MOF@PA samples were mixed with a certain amount of deionized water and ground to form paste. Then, the paste was spin-coated onto the surface of ceramic substrates form homogeneous sensing films. The flexible humidity sensor was fabricated by the same method using polyethylene terephthalate (PET) interdigital electrode (IDE) as the flexible substrate. The fabrication method of humidity sensors is similar to that in our reported works [24,25].
The humidity sensing measurements were carried out at room temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . The current signals were recorded by CHI760E electrochemical analyzer under a constant applied voltage (with $3\mathrm{~V~}$ DC potential). This method of measuring current is common in other reported works [26,27]. The RH atmospheres were produced by different saturated salt solutions in their equilibrium states including LiCl for $11\%$ RH, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ for $23\%$ RH, ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{CO}}_{3}$ for $43\%$ RH, $\mathsf{M g}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ for $54\%$ RH, NaCl for $75\%$ RH, KCl for $85\%$ RH, ${\mathrm{KNO}}_{3}$ for $95\%$ RH at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ [28,29]. The response of sensors is defined as: $I_{g}/I_{o}$ , where $I_{g}$ and $I_{o}$ indicate the current of sensors in different saturated salt solutions and $11\%$ RH, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and morphology characterization
The 2D Co-MOF and 2D Co-MOF@PA samples were prepared by simple and facile hydrothermal reactions without templates, as shown in Scheme 1. Subsequently, a series of material characterization techniques were performed to analyze the surface morphologies, size and microstructures of the samples. At first, the TEM images of 2D Co-MOF are shown in Fig. 1. 2D Co-MOF nanosheets present a square flake structure with a smooth surface with the average side length of about $0.75~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 1a and b). The unique paper-like structure of the 2D Co-MOF is conducive to functional modification and adsorption of water molecules. The EDS mapping analysis of the elements C, N and Co (Fig. 1d-f) is carried out by choosing a 2D Co-MOF nanosheet from TEM images (Fig. 1c). There is no doubt that the uniform distribution of C, N and Co is observed throughout the surface of Co-MOF nanosheet. After modification of PA, the corresponding TEM images and EDS element mapping of 2D Co-MOF@PA nanosheets are shown in Fig. 2. The flakes with tiny scale are interwoven and spread across the surface of Co-MOF@PA nanosheets (Fig. 2a and b). Compared with the structure of Co-MOF nanosheets, the surface of Co$\mathsf{M O F@P A}$ becomes obviously rough.
Moreover, another element (P) appears, which is consistent with the elements appeared from XPS analysis below. The surface chemical compositions of the Co-MOF and Co-MOF@PA are evaluated by XPS as given in Fig. S1. The Co-MOF@PA is composed of C, N, O, Co and P elements. In Fig. 3a, the Co 2p characteristic peaks consist of two parts, Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathtt{C o2p}_{1/2}$ [30]. It is found that the Co 2p peaks of ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ are obviously weaker than that of Co-MOF. This phenomenon is related to the coating of Co-MOF by PA. In Fig. 3b, the high-resolution C 1s XPS spectrum is ascribed to $\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{C}(\sim284.2),\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N}(\sim285.6\mathrm{eV})$ and CAO ( 288.1 eV) [31].
The N 1s spectrum of Co-MOF can be mainly ascribed to pyrrolic-N $(401.6\mathrm{eV})$ [24], as shown in Fig. 3c. However, Co-MOF@PA features two characteristic peaks, corresponding to pyrrolic-N (399.6 eV) and pyridinic-N (397.8 eV) [32]. The core level spectrum of P 2s and $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ peaks at approximate $191\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $133.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ validates the existence of PA in Co-MOF@PA in Fig. S1 [33]. The highresolution $\mathtt{P2p}$ spectrum of ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ shown in Fig. 3d can be assigned as the core levels of $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ atoms in phosphate at $133.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P-O-Co}$ at 134.1 eV [34], further confirming the successful grafting of PA in Co-MOF.
In the Raman spectrum of Co-MOF $@$ PA (Fig. S2), the peak of Co$\mathsf{M O F@P A}$ at $383~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the Co-O stretching modes. The sharp peaks identified in $1502\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1542\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ connected with $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching vibration. The peaks in $553~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to $_{0-\mathsf{P}-0}$ bending of the phosphoric and phosphonate groups. The peaks appear at $1366~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1230\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the stretch of $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and OACAO [35,36].
## 3.2. Humidity sensing performances
In order to investigate the humidity sensing performance, saturated salt solutions are introduced to configure different humidity conditions for testing, as shown in Fig. 4. Fig. 5a illustrates the variation of responsive current signals under different RH (from $23\%$ to $95\%$ RH). It is worth noting that the Co-MOF sensor is almost unresponsive to moisture in the range of 11– $85\%$ RH. Until the humidity reaches a relatively high value of $95\%$ RH, the current signal of CoMOF sensor shows a sudden change and indicates significantly increased response. This is because the metal ion building units of MOF have metal sites with incomplete coordination, which can intensely contact with plenty of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules [34]. The result suggests that Co-MOF has hydrophilicity and a better proton conduction at high humidity. Consequently, it is difficult for the pure Co-MOF sensor to monitor different humidity continuously. In order to create continuous dynamic response at various humidity levels, we fabricated a novel Co-MOF based sensor coated with a hydrophilic coating layer of PA. PA is an ecologically friendly, renewable and abundant biomass, which is easily gained from grains. The six phosphate groups supply all kinds of feasible crosslinking sites that may ‘‘suture” many conducting polymers sheets, forming the three-dimensional assembly body [37]. In contrast, the response and recovery curves of Co-MOF@PA sensor are given in Fig. 5b under different humidity levels. As we know, the response and recovery time can be defined as the current change reaching $90\%$ . Herein, the response time and recovery time of our Co-MOF $@$ PA-based humidity sensors towards $54\%$ RH are 179 s and 27 s, respectively. As RH increases from $23\%$ to $95\%$ RH, the current values of Co-MOF@PA sensor grow exponentially (Fig. 5c). Meanwhile, the response of Co-MOF@PA sensor to different humidity shows varying degrees of change, and e ven responses as high as 2250 at $95\%$ RH (Fig. 5d), indicating that CoMOF@PA sensor has excellent sensitivity in both high and low humidity environment. Especially, the sensing performances of humidity sensors based on Co-MOF@PA are compared with other reported humidity sensors based on carbon materials, polymers, metal oxides and other MOF composites [38–43]. The CoMOF@PA sensor has higher response, as shown in the Table S1. Indeed, When PA is attached to the surface of Co-MOF, the molecule consisting of six phosphate groups releases free protons that can etch the framework of MOF to enhance the hydrophilicity of Co-MOF [32,44]. Under a low RH condition $11-54\%$ RH), small amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are adsorbed on the surface of Co${\mathrm{MOF@PA}}$ , forming a layer or several layers of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules, leading to discontinuous proton conduction at low RH [5]. When RH is up to $75-95\%$ , proton conduction becomes easier. Moreover, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ ions are easily generated by the chemical reactions, including $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}0+\mathrm{H}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{3}0^{+}$ , $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{3}0^{+}+{\mathrm{OH}}^{-})$ 1 [45–49], resulting in better ionic conductivity. Hence, Co-MOF@PA humidity sensor has a large response at high RH. It can be obtained that the Co-MOF@PA humidity sensor shows an obvious response during the rapid switching process of RH, which makes it attractive in environment monitoring.
The repeatability and stability are important parameters to evaluate the performance of humidity sensor. Fig. 6a exhibits the typical I-V curves under different RH $(43-95\%)$ . When the voltage varies in the range from $-3\mathrm{~V~}$ to $_{3\vee}$ the slope of the curve goes up as the RH raises, indicating that the electrical conductivity of ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ increases as the increase of humidity. The result is caused by the formation of hydrogen bond networks between the water and sensing materials [50,51]. As the water molecules attach to the surface of materials, the number of protonconducting hydrogen bonds increases to form 3D network. Notably, the proton current of ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ at $85\%$ RH is thirtyfold higher than that at $43\%$ RH. At $95\%$ RH, more water was adsorbed on Co-MOF@PA film, forming more hydrogen-bonded proton channels, which increased proton transfer, thus resulting in the sharp change in current [51,52]. Besides, the change of responsive current maintain a similar amplitude towards $54\%$ RH in the five response and recovery cycles, indicating Co-MOF@PA sensor exhibits the superior repeatability (Fig. 6b). The stability performance of the humidity sensors is further validated with exposure $54\%$ RH for a month. As shown in Fig. 6c, the humidity response of
Co-MOF@PA sensor to $54\%$ RH only demonstrates small fluctuations during this period, which is consistent with the good stability of the sensor.
Furthermore, we explore the performance of flexible sensors based on Co-MOF@PA, which fabricate on flexible PET substrates. The consecutive response and recovery properties of the flexible humidity sensor based on Co-MOF@PA between $11\%$ RH to $75\%$ RH are presented in Fig. 7a. No obvious degradation occurs in the sensing performance during continuous 9 cycles, indicating superior reproducibility of the flexible sensors. Additionally, bending test has been performed to evaluate the repeatability of the flexible sensor under $45\%$ Fig. 7b. In order to characterize the performance of the sensor under different mechanical deformations, the initial current under different bending angles was measured. As shown in Fig. 7c, when the sensor is bent at different angles, its current change is almost negligible, which showing potential wearable applications. The superior sensing performance of the flexible humidity sensors based on ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ allows their practical applications, such as non-contact switch, fruit freshness test and moisturizing effect of cosmetics evaluation. As shown in Fig. 7d, when a wet fingertip approaches the sensor, the current raises due to the increase in the RH level around the sensor. When the distance increases from $1\ \mathrm{mm}$ to $5\mathrm{mm}$ , the current decreases significantly, indicating the high sensitivity for non-contact humidity sensing. In addition, the moisturizing effect of cosmetics can also be evaluated by the ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ sensors as depicted in Fig. 7e. Five different brands of cosmetics were evenly applied on the back of the hand, and moisturizing effect was measured after half an hour. Afterwards, whether the fruit surface is coated with wax or not is investigated by the humidity sensor herein. As shown in Fig. 7f, the humidity response of unwaxed apples or oranges is higher, while this of waxed apples or oranges is lower.
The state of the human body and different health conditions can cause changes in breathing rate and depth. Here, we studied its potential application in human respiratory frequency monitoring by fixing the humidity sensor on the mask to conveniently measure the respiratory frequency. The photographs of the wireless circuit board and the flow chart of the monitoring system, as shown in Fig. 8a and b, respectively. The humidity sensor is mounted in the circuit board. Four breathing states were simulated and recorded with Co-MOF@PA sensor. The RH change of the ambient environment in real time is displayed via wireless system we designed. Fig. 8c-f show the real-time display of humidity via mobile phone software under different humidity condition. The sensor can distinguish slow breathing, deep breathing, normal breathing and fast breathing changes in breathing frequency and depth. The each cycle of four breathing states is approximately 5.1s, 7.4 s, 3.8 s and $1.7~\mathsf{s}.$ Thus, ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@P A}}$ sensor has potential applications in personal conditional monitoring and diagnostic breath analysis.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, we prepared a flexible, non-contact and multifunctional Co-MOF@PA-based humidity sensor. Compared with pure Co-MOF-based senor, the response range and sensitivity of Co-MOF@PA-based senor have been greatly improved. The humidity sensor has an excellent humidity sensing response over $2\times10^{3}$ and good stability, while the humidity range can span $23\text{\textperthousand}$ RH.
The Co-MOF@PA sensor is used to successfully realize real-time facial respiration monitoring, skin humidity perception, cosmetic moisturizing evaluation, fruit freshness testing, etc., paving the way for next-generation commercially body and environment monitoring.
